1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,850
This moth is in a museum, because it got stuck inside a computer.

2
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:06,147
On September ninth, 1947,

3
00:00:06,147 --> 00:00:12,040
engineers working on the Harvard Mark II found it trapped in relay number seventy.

4
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:14,564
The Mark II was built from relays,

5
00:00:14,564 --> 00:00:17,880
little switches that clicked open and shut with magnets.

6
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:20,841
One moth in the wrong place, and a circuit failed.

7
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:23,967
So they taped it into the logbook, and wrote:

8
00:00:23,967 --> 00:00:26,119
First actual case of bug being found.

9
00:00:26,617 --> 00:00:30,380
But the joke only works because bug was already slang.

10
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:34,074
Thomas Edison was calling glitches bugs back in 1878.

11
00:00:34,583 --> 00:00:39,533
Grace Hopper, who worked on the Mark II, loved telling the story and made it famous.

12
00:00:39,533 --> 00:00:42,067
The logbook page is now at the Smithsonian.

13
00:00:42,583 --> 00:00:44,981
Three months later, the transistor arrived.

14
00:00:44,981 --> 00:00:46,152
What if it never had?

15
00:00:46,152 --> 00:00:47,601
Full story on the channel.
